    "display_name": "Alice in Wonderland",
    "long_instructions": "Find n-grams in a sentence from *Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland* by Lewis Carroll, where `n` can be any number you choose.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n- Complete the `get_ngrams` function. Given a list of tokens, and a value for `n` the function should return a list of n-grams.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nA list slice like `tokens[1:4]` grabs the items from index 1 up to (but not including) index 4, and gives them back as a new list.\r\n\r\n```\r\ntokens = [\"Alice\", \"was\", \"beginning\", \"to\"]\r\nprint(tokens[1:3])    # [\"was\", \"beginning\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd the `tuple` function can be used to turn a `list` into a `tuple`.\r\n\r\n```\r\ntokens = [\"Alice\", \"was\", \"beginning\", \"to\"]\r\nprint(tuple(tokens))    # (\"Alice\", \"was\", \"beginning\", \"to\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo these can be combined to create a tuple of a slice of a list:\r\n\r\n```\r\ntokens = [\"Alice\", \"was\", \"beginning\", \"to\"]\r\nprint(tuple(tokens[1:3]))    # (\"was\", \"beginning\")\r\n```",
